evidence.claim:
Claude: **must-fix**: This fix does not actually prevent the out-of-bounds read it targets: the `cw < 0` guard here is always false, so the `p + 1` branch is dead code and the advance always falls through to `utf8_next_char(p)` — the exact blind skip the PR sets out to avoid.

Control flow in the loop body:

```c
int cw = utf8_char_console_width(p);
if (cw < 0) {
        log_debug_errno(cw, "Comment to line break contains invalid UTF-8, ignoring.");
        cw = 1;          /* cw is unconditionally clamped to 1 here */
}
...
p = cw < 0 ? p + 1 : utf8_next_char(p);   /* cw is now guaranteed >= 1 */
```

By the time this line runs, `cw` is never negative (valid widths are 1/2/8, and the invalid case was reset to 1). For the added test input `"foo\xF0"` (bytes `66 6F 6F F0 00`), when `p` points at `0xF0`, `utf8_char_console_width()` returns negative, `cw` becomes 1, and `utf8_next_char(p)` advances by `utf8_skip_data[0xF0] == 4`, stepping over the NUL; the next `*p != 0` test reads past the end of the allocation.

The new regression tests only assert the return code and string equality — the over-read is silent and would only trip under ASan/valgrind, so they do not actually catch the regression.

Capture the invalid-UTF-8 condition before `cw` is clamped, e.g.:

```c
int cw = utf8_char_console_width(p);
bool invalid = cw < 0;
if (invalid) {
        log_debug_errno(cw, "Comment to line break contains invalid UTF-8, ignoring.");
        cw = 1;
}
...
p = invalid ? p + 1 : utf8_next_char(p);
```

